#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068532 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee633017-5c17-575f-bdab-5f12b7319355" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Area Between Two Curves as an Integral Given the Limits of Integration"}] :question/id #uuid "481f1075-efc3-546a-a1f3-9415f7fc642d" :question/math-academy-id "e-552" :question/problem "Write down an integral that gives the area of the finite region bounded by the curves $y=x^2$ and $y=\\sqrt x,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/b98474e6de2c94876a75758d56a7fb356de22bccd029bfeed0207affebab09ce.png)" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the intersection points between the two graphs occur when $x=0$ and $x=1.$ So these will be our limits of integration.\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/1b1c2b1826fa988ba23f7270b4c8bbdc91bf03066e8ca3638def329466d5b93e.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=x^2,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=\\sqrt x.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\sqrt{x})-({x}^{2})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\sqrt{x}-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068533 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8fa1bebf-4af0-5a5c-962c-8a88f8392776" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Area Between Two Curves Expressed as Functions of X"}] :question/id #uuid "ff023940-30f8-5b30-ab1b-9ca1f335a6a8" :question/math-academy-id "e-553" :question/problem "Find the area of the finite region enclosed between $f(x) =x^2-2x+2$ and $g(x) = 2,$ shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/01f139c53b6328341b463f5ad941d37fccb241fff109c0584207017341e8e5f7.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2x+2 & =2 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x & =0 \\\\ x(x-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=2.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, we draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/39798ef4ff6e898527864628dc2750f88b115c03b2d6b8fb729f30b21d2d2bf5.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=x^2-2x+2,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=2.$\n\nTherefore, we can find the area as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[\\,(2)-({x}^{2}-2x+2)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(-{x}^{2}+2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(2x-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={({x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3})|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =({2}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {2}^{3})-(0-0) \\\\  & =(4-\\frac{8}{3})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068534 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e016cedd-0206-555c-8934-c6d19566d547" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Area Between Two Curves as an Integral by First Solving For the Limits of Integration"}] :question/id #uuid "d1c021cd-b537-5504-8836-eb7a59ba86b6" :question/math-academy-id "e-554" :question/problem "What is the integral that gives the area of the finite region bounded by $y=x^2+x+5$ and $y=x+9?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw a sketch of the two functions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/5f6980e3a7e4c7d1a5cc5d9b93a0110764b34ea6b7a4bec7c828f04cef842f7f.png)\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+x+5 & =x+9 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)(x+2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-2$ to $x=2.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/3c52f1405079380459e60ad35e05218d1f0468ee177f1e383498c672d48eaa8d.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=x^2+x+5,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=x+9.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}[\\,(x+9)-({x}^{2}+x+5)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}(4-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291482 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e016cedd-0206-555c-8934-c6d19566d547" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Area Between Two Curves as an Integral by First Solving For the Limits of Integration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cfac9120-eb2d-5507-b553-90da7e76cd63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{4}(5+4x-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291484} {:answer/id #uuid "15b73c36-3bdc-5603-8ee1-16a746763542" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{5}(5+4x-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291485} {:answer/id #uuid "fc78b8a5-decd-5153-98a2-48f1e78f2d67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{4}({x}^{2}-4x-5)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291486} {:answer/id #uuid "5ffc81ca-faa8-5d1c-bb8a-2c0898e4c2fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{5}({x}^{2}-4x-5)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291487} {:answer/id #uuid "24197187-5ffd-5de5-adcb-49c45ff22388" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{5}({x}^{2}-4x-5)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291488}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15b73c36-3bdc-5603-8ee1-16a746763542" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{5}(5+4x-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291485} :answer-field/id #uuid "16e9d5af-1c25-5005-9231-15a7611d204a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291483}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dd8d0f5b-37a6-5193-9c93-2abd275a5141" :question/math-academy-id "q-11487" :question/problem "Which integral gives the area of the finite region bounded between $y={x}^{2}-5$ and $y=4x?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw a sketch of the two functions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/b25bee7ef7257a5611d708eb65b3c8b453b230ad6d2e9289f6cb40dffba89277.png)\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x & ={x}^{2}-5 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x-5 & =0 \\\\ (x+1)(x-5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-1$ to $x=5.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/bf79cec9c1ede4a95b3e20415fbf37224ca8616426e8530043fa4a4b54780bab.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y={x}^{2}-5,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=4x.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{5}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{5}[\\,(4x)-({x}^{2}-5)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{5}(-{x}^{2}+4x+5)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{5}(5+4x-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291489 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8fa1bebf-4af0-5a5c-962c-8a88f8392776" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Area Between Two Curves Expressed as Functions of X"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "707b79b8-625e-5731-ac5e-d62db9e56406" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291491} {:answer/id #uuid "9feb6521-4acd-5629-b935-c878f5ed613c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291492} {:answer/id #uuid "0f0603b0-b608-5b2d-a2e2-9263798a3e11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291493} {:answer/id #uuid "d890a635-43b5-5954-bc9c-0b6ecb2eaa4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291494} {:answer/id #uuid "197a676d-a3fa-5e3f-ab32-4e6eabf55b56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291495}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9feb6521-4acd-5629-b935-c878f5ed613c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291492} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5afbd31-dfe4-5306-a41f-19a0229cf16a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291490}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d7acbd35-2c0b-57d1-af2a-99344b438f27" :question/math-academy-id "q-11488" :question/problem "Find the area of the finite region enclosed between $y=4{x}^{2}-1$ and $y=3{x}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw a sketch of the two functions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/4e3758ddf652c3dd7df10d381b42ca1dde50d48250bed672b740329191202597.png)\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2} & =4{x}^{2}-1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-1$ to $x=1.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/54ad3b4c8bc3aef707af764cd8d8badf9390737dc75491aa66ea554f531a7576.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=4{x}^{2}-1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=3{x}^{2}.$\n\nWe can now set up the integration with our $x$ values (Top-Bottom):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[\\,(3{x}^{2})-(4{x}^{2}-1)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the integrand is an even function. Therefore, we can find the area, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}(1-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{(x-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3})|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2((1-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {1}^{3})-0) \\\\  & =2(1-\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291496 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee633017-5c17-575f-bdab-5f12b7319355" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Area Between Two Curves as an Integral Given the Limits of Integration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "149ea9a1-7db3-5b03-a2d2-ec226205574b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}(-{x}^{2}+3)\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291498} {:answer/id #uuid "1dfd2477-7fa0-58bf-af28-2b8481fa73bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{3}(-{x}^{2}-2x-1)\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291499} {:answer/id #uuid "b7921985-5e30-56c0-82bd-d7e5af928e64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-3}^{1}(-{x}^{2}-2x+3)\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291500} {:answer/id #uuid "c4f84890-1d1f-5f74-a8f9-80c770005867" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-3}^{1}({x}^{2}+x+3)\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291501} {:answer/id #uuid "c374d4f8-6c79-568e-992d-9d1c0a03e868" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-3}^{1}({x}^{2}-3x)\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291502}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7921985-5e30-56c0-82bd-d7e5af928e64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-3}^{1}(-{x}^{2}-2x+3)\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291500} :answer-field/id #uuid "4fb38d7d-3f02-5a95-9252-236f17f95c63" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291497}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6c05958a-e92d-52eb-a776-84210c48e5f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-120871" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/0ac836789132a294051f6cbd0756f26407d002fa83a278b3b8825cfdd9af0b81.png)\n\nWhich integral gives the area of the finite region bounded by $y={x}^{2}+3x-2$ and $y=x+1,$ shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the intersection points between the two graphs occur when $x=-3$ and $x=1.$ So these will be our limits of integration.\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/299608a54c0b5f568f32619722dd678af1388065f017620071ffb0473a686fa3.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y={x}^{2}+3x-2,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=x+1.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-3}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{1}[\\,(x+1)-({x}^{2}+3x-2)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{1}(-{x}^{2}-2x+3)\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291503 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee633017-5c17-575f-bdab-5f12b7319355" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Area Between Two Curves as an Integral Given the Limits of Integration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c11c3bf8-312f-5a70-9233-c53d2aa0cf46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1/4}^{3/4}(-8{x}^{2}-2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291505} {:answer/id #uuid "1a332f33-85b0-5324-8301-b44ff93699c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{3/4}(-8{x}^{2}+x+5)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291506} {:answer/id #uuid "0378c0f2-21d1-51ed-aaf0-ebd978ab2497" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1/3}^{3/4}(-{x}^{2}-2x+6)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291507} {:answer/id #uuid "25877345-fe31-505f-9cb2-97dd7779a860" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{3/4}(8{x}^{2}-x-4)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291508} {:answer/id #uuid "b30f0b69-53ad-5c42-bc9b-a047fcdeaf31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{3/4}(-8{x}^{2}-2x+6)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291509}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b30f0b69-53ad-5c42-bc9b-a047fcdeaf31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{3/4}(-8{x}^{2}-2x+6)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291509} :answer-field/id #uuid "35da2a72-17f3-52f4-9887-3a1a23d8be77" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291504}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8b0cb8d7-3e11-5e09-bfb8-3963a1210c8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-121424" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/a928b14cfe88f9aaf7b8cf5288a18f3e81e163ea65fd3a48653fcba630c11386.png)\n\nWhich integral gives the area of the finite region bounded by $y=8{x}^{2}-5$ and $y=1-2x,$ shown above?\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the intersection points between the two graphs occur when $x=-1$ and $x=\\frac{3}{4}.$ So these will be our limits of integration.\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/226ade4a3b546484ba2e9ce5f266391fb51e9fe4b62e2e78d12200ac54ecea9a.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=8{x}^{2}-5,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=1-2x.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{3/4}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{3/4}[\\,(1-2x)-(8{x}^{2}-5)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{3/4}(-8{x}^{2}-2x+6)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291510 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e016cedd-0206-555c-8934-c6d19566d547" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Area Between Two Curves as an Integral by First Solving For the Limits of Integration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18287057-ea0a-518c-9691-c7d0408a9617" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{6/5}(-5{x}^{2}+5x-6)\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291512} {:answer/id #uuid "08d2a3d0-fefc-5d13-86e4-568953213391" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{6/5}(-5{x}^{2}+x+6)\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291513} {:answer/id #uuid "ebb25768-4002-514b-bbdf-1848601fdcc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1/5}({x}^{2}+x+3)\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291514} {:answer/id #uuid "372b9448-e262-5710-a406-4a4fd99da531" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{2/5}(5{x}^{2}+x+4)\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291515} {:answer/id #uuid "d6683605-25f4-534a-9e56-69e2d3a9e473" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1/5}^{6/5}(-5{x}^{2}-x+2)\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291516}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08d2a3d0-fefc-5d13-86e4-568953213391" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{6/5}(-5{x}^{2}+x+6)\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291513} :answer-field/id #uuid "e70b1c5b-29e7-59b7-b280-86a6990dddb5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291511}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d3a05e1c-a69f-56e3-b571-e32c1197e412" :question/math-academy-id "q-121512" :question/problem "Which integral gives the area of the finite region bounded by $y=3{x}^{2}-5x$ and $y=6-2{x}^{2}-4x?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw a sketch of the two functions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/813677aa1b12481e990058b129cb9ad24945a3dfb818afacfb2ecb720d6b81d3.png)\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-5x & =6-2{x}^{2}-4x \\\\ 5{x}^{2}-x-6 & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1\\pm \\sqrt{(-1)^{2}-4\\cdot 5\\cdot (-6)}}{2\\cdot 5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\pm 11}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-1$ to $x=\\frac{6}{5}.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/a8c538d5f40f68506588695aa04b38f095360b6f51369d8169099806a9678de9.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=3{x}^{2}-5x,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=6-2{x}^{2}-4x.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{6/5}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{6/5}[\\,(6-2{x}^{2}-4x)-(3{x}^{2}-5x)\\,]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{6/5}(-5{x}^{2}+x+6)\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291517 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e016cedd-0206-555c-8934-c6d19566d547" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Area Between Two Curves as an Integral by First Solving For the Limits of Integration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2392df36-67f9-5e27-a0c6-6b0026c175f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{4}(4-{x}^{2})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291519} {:answer/id #uuid "fae4c85b-01f7-585f-9179-04329a1fbb59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-4}^{4}(16+4{x}^{2})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291520} {:answer/id #uuid "a3647819-fcc7-5680-861d-bd11d552a8a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-4}^{4}(16-{x}^{2})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291521} {:answer/id #uuid "3f8bb39b-43c1-5fbd-a398-407183920383" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{2}(8-{x}^{2})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291522} {:answer/id #uuid "71d53f25-d523-54c4-a780-b7ab3274cfef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-4}^{1}({x}^{2}-4)\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291523}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3647819-fcc7-5680-861d-bd11d552a8a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-4}^{4}(16-{x}^{2})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291521} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b339274-4f6a-5f2b-92df-78c9f65fe43b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291518}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f1267085-eeb4-5b23-9c27-dc0dee8c5147" :question/math-academy-id "q-121526" :question/problem "Which integral gives the area of the finite region bounded between $y={x}^{2}+2x-9$ and $y=2x+7?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the graphs of the two functions:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/2514c3e7bf5352dbe26dddc479f66042b3736b93f82b97a60850f768a89a4e17.png)\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2x-9 & =2x+7 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =16 \\\\ x & =\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-4$ to $x=4.$\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/560fb0937828d5afb5de9593280a1177773598c5d3534ee26318e0bc2788f5ab.png)\n\n \n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y={x}^{2}+2x-9,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=2x+7.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-4}^{4}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-4}^{4}[\\,(2x+7)-({x}^{2}+2x-9)\\,]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-4}^{4}(-{x}^{2}+16)\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-4}^{4}(16-{x}^{2})\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291524 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8fa1bebf-4af0-5a5c-962c-8a88f8392776" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Area Between Two Curves Expressed as Functions of X"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f11d683b-c1a7-5ea2-a1b2-44e326c96f65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291526} {:answer/id #uuid "599e765b-9113-5edf-a098-b794cd8acf05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{250}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291527} {:answer/id #uuid "c05dfeb1-ce69-557d-b5e6-784020e18874" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291528} {:answer/id #uuid "32667257-77f9-5c35-9ad5-917ddd15e307" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{256}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291529} {:answer/id #uuid "dad44faf-cc92-54f9-a63d-8f7f63e83ce3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{157}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291530}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "32667257-77f9-5c35-9ad5-917ddd15e307" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{256}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291529} :answer-field/id #uuid "887f178a-f341-58f2-a1a5-1775c0807647" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291525}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "49edfe25-0508-5110-9496-2725f5f95615" :question/math-academy-id "q-121580" :question/problem "Calculate the area of the finite region bounded by the curves $y={x}^{2}-9$ and $y=7.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw a sketch of the two functions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/85961197a76cc38713537f80a9c97bb158247c71a8be4633c98245ea158634b4.png)\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-9 & =7 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =16 \\\\ x & =\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-4$ to $x=4.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, we draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/7543876e9138be135630313317e162c9b256713f2565b0cd3b5a24bb9ba9a3b3.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y={x}^{2}-9,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=7.$\n\nTherefore, we can find the area, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-4}^{4}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-4}^{4}[\\,(7)-({x}^{2}-9)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-4}^{4}(16-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(16x-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3})|}_{-4}^{4} \\\\  & =[16\\cdot 4-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {4}^{3}]-[16\\cdot (-4)-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-4)^{3}] \\\\  & =(64-\\frac{64}{3})-(-64+\\frac{64}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{256}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291531 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee633017-5c17-575f-bdab-5f12b7319355" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Area Between Two Curves as an Integral Given the Limits of Integration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f68fe51f-a4d9-5f8f-aa10-f2e8fa70fec9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1/2}^{1}(x-1+2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291533} {:answer/id #uuid "3c29da1f-9850-5b4d-a908-1c5101e99420" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1/2}^{1}(x+1-2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291534} {:answer/id #uuid "3af74cc8-e984-5307-932a-d1e2de0ecbe2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1/2}^{1}(2{x}^{2}-x-1)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291535} {:answer/id #uuid "4f880dfe-cf92-5630-a407-fb84613d0779" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1/2}^{1}(2{x}^{2}+x-1)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291536} {:answer/id #uuid "5b1ec612-898d-50c9-bb3c-0d699ac52d41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1/2}^{1}(x-1-2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291537}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c29da1f-9850-5b4d-a908-1c5101e99420" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1/2}^{1}(x+1-2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291534} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d27a92d-a819-5dbc-8fab-b0aa6bc376dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291532}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c69be2ea-7b70-598d-bb9a-251384a82770" :question/math-academy-id "q-3121" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/9a87ae2b230e66b685051ee7d13987f5f68690a07a67ade9e7af0eb870f6224f.png)\n\nWhich integral gives the area of the finite region bounded by $y=2{x}^{2}$ and $y=x+1,$ as shown above?\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the intersection points between the two graphs occur when $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=1.$ So these will be our limits of integration.\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/e77170aac6d81b1cfd0e93804b0c59802f28089d24aeaff065da04cabc44c172.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=2{x}^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=x+1.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-1/2}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1/2}^{1}[\\,(x+1)-(2{x}^{2})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1/2}^{1}(x+1-2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291538 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8fa1bebf-4af0-5a5c-962c-8a88f8392776" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Area Between Two Curves Expressed as Functions of X"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a09d3698-cee1-5a99-8f63-6d930b6b8555" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291540} {:answer/id #uuid "66e4c855-4581-5a3a-91b4-ea2c82c9b481" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291541} {:answer/id #uuid "49511dfd-2f1a-5934-9ab9-5d081ce4476d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291542} {:answer/id #uuid "9009f62a-8f9f-541f-aaa9-3096b237215e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291543} {:answer/id #uuid "a9694e31-220c-5016-a56c-a6d1ed8cbfa5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291544}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a09d3698-cee1-5a99-8f63-6d930b6b8555" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291540} :answer-field/id #uuid "534945ed-8eee-5cf5-bf23-7e34d5e5fac3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291539}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5da2e5f1-1de9-5fc2-b99b-9668203d66cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-3146" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/c61d9140e5b8e57c8f75b532c6e26e22faa073e6631984a8f6a7b6f4280882e3.png)\n\nFind the area of the finite region enclosed between $y={x}^{2}+4x$ and $y=x,$ shown above.\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*" :question/worked-solution "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+4x & =x \\\\ {x}^{2}+3x & =0 \\\\ x(x+3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-3$ to $x=0.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, we draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/6b2232d5a2853b22c731c317b365822e371f3d419bfcb3e528a07f211a372553.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y={x}^{2}+4x,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=x.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-3}^{0}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{0}[\\,(x)-({x}^{2}+4x)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{0}(-{x}^{2}-3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2})|}_{-3}^{0} \\\\  & =[-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {0}^{3}-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {0}^{2}]-[-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-3)^{3}-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot (-3)^{2}] \\\\  & =(0)-(9-\\frac{27}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291545 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8fa1bebf-4af0-5a5c-962c-8a88f8392776" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Area Between Two Curves Expressed as Functions of X"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4d18c80-2a7e-5607-9570-a2b1ce6478ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{256}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291547}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4d18c80-2a7e-5607-9570-a2b1ce6478ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{256}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291547} :answer-field/id #uuid "0029ae7e-f00e-594c-aaab-b3d91b97df04" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291546}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bbf20fe3-aec0-56b8-9300-f9d1b858cbfb" :question/math-academy-id "q-346621" :question/problem "Find the area $A$ of the finite region bounded by the curves $y={x}^{2}-5$ and $y=11.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw a sketch of the two functions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/d2398c3a8a04d005dd4d0c6e8b06046a6ca98aa86127204e7521341213dfb464.png)\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-5 & =11 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =16 \\\\ x & =\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-4$ to $x=4.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, we draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/caf1c4d5df49aa18ed85158615be19745abb7c078269dde35f4e57d57fae292a.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y={x}^{2}-5,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=11.$\n\nTherefore, we can find the area as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-4}^{4}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-4}^{4}[\\,(11)-({x}^{2}-5)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-4}^{4}(16-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(16x-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3})|}_{-4}^{4} \\\\  & =[16\\cdot 4-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {4}^{3}]-[16\\cdot (-4)-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-4)^{3}] \\\\  & =(64-\\frac{64}{3})-(-64+\\frac{64}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{256}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291548 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8fa1bebf-4af0-5a5c-962c-8a88f8392776" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Area Between Two Curves Expressed as Functions of X"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bfed83e3-a255-594e-a670-b12bc21afbf5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291550}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bfed83e3-a255-594e-a670-b12bc21afbf5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291550} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d39581b-30e9-596a-af6d-072df58968c5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291549}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b345d94e-b06b-524d-aa98-24568478ff38" :question/math-academy-id "q-351944" :question/problem "Find the area $A$ of the finite region bounded by the curves $y={x}^{2}+6$ and $y=10.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw a sketch of the two functions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/5ba7f7b4022c35c4afe7d285c57f87d6fcebfbcd8373ddc530f849e29fbc22de.png)\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+6 & =10 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4 \\\\ x & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-2$ to $x=2.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, we draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/3ff79baa7d3d934dd9815b398cbd9f56990011370408ddede51e3c4110b4978c.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y={x}^{2}+6,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=10.$\n\nTherefore, we can find the area as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}[\\,(10)-({x}^{2}+6)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}(4-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(4x-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3})|}_{-2}^{2} \\\\  & =[4\\cdot 2-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {2}^{3}]-[4\\cdot (-2)-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-2)^{3}] \\\\  & =(8-\\frac{8}{3})-(-8+\\frac{8}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291551 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e016cedd-0206-555c-8934-c6d19566d547" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Area Between Two Curves as an Integral by First Solving For the Limits of Integration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f60f4407-4c88-511d-891a-c3cffb94fb8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{4/3}(3x-2)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291553} {:answer/id #uuid "7f6fdf07-4470-58e0-83c6-bec3fccf1cde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}(2{x}^{2}-6x+4)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291554} {:answer/id #uuid "92f35698-fbf6-554a-a6d4-e3192b4628c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{4/3}^{2}(2{x}^{2}-3x+2)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291555} {:answer/id #uuid "f9e13dbf-c306-58bc-a7e5-353ad7533ee4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}(-2{x}^{2}+6x-4)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291556} {:answer/id #uuid "64680e23-c2ad-5159-9b07-d9ab2b534e74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{4/3}(2{x}^{2}-x-2)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291557}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9e13dbf-c306-58bc-a7e5-353ad7533ee4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}(-2{x}^{2}+6x-4)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291556} :answer-field/id #uuid "508d5843-753f-5e35-b011-e4f47b56bb91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291552}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b895499b-b902-5259-aaab-68a4f2e466b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-49800" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/b5e422ab61b4ebcacafcadc082a1b85ebb453a689654a9cef7bc7e7a3a4721a3.png)\n\nWhich integral gives the area of the finite region bounded by $y=f(x)=2{x}^{2}-3x+2$ and $y=g(x)=3x-2$, shown above?\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*" :question/worked-solution "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-3x+2 & =3x-2 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-6x+4 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-3x+2 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)(x-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=1$ to $x=2.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, we draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/7d96f51f01c2e52756fe53a6b3a11dd5c7c6dd9cb15ce674a706b442a3e6ad2b.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $f(x)=2{x}^{2}-3x+2,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $g(x)=3x-2.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}[\\,(3x-2)-(2{x}^{2}-3x+2)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(-2{x}^{2}+6x-4)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291558 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee633017-5c17-575f-bdab-5f12b7319355" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Area Between Two Curves as an Integral Given the Limits of Integration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c9b9e3b5-1d8a-5778-9ade-73b2ac6b7e86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-4}^{8}(-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+x+8)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291560} {:answer/id #uuid "53848356-a06a-5fe2-ab1e-50d4c90e64d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-4}^{8}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-2)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291561} {:answer/id #uuid "4f63bde5-58e2-5d40-874e-044bd20c6e3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{4}({x}^{2}+x+8)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291562} {:answer/id #uuid "1cfb3d58-3d5e-5c79-9366-227dbad3db20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-4}^{8}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-x-8)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291563} {:answer/id #uuid "a7ef0012-cb76-560a-9970-0e43b64287fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{4}(x+6)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291564}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9b9e3b5-1d8a-5778-9ade-73b2ac6b7e86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-4}^{8}(-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+x+8)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291560} :answer-field/id #uuid "24b9eb88-d29f-5222-a148-5ec7f854d5aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291559}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "72208a61-59e2-5d43-bb42-bed25d98e203" :question/math-academy-id "q-49880" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/1e75b221cacd3d2bad25065fe13bbf77f07fa711fe7f865358ff181039ced560.png)\n\nWhich integral gives the area of the finite region bounded by $y=x+6$ and $y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-2$, shown above?\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the intersection points between the two graphs occur when $x=-4$ and $x=8.$ So these will be our limits of integration.\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/56ec4a3e3bd4b8b652832d4b32ba730717a49ab2438ef2c978ea99b291a7f462.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $g(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-2,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $f(x)=x+6.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-4}^{8}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-4}^{8}[\\,(x+6)-(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-2)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-4}^{8}(-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+x+8)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069313 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e016cedd-0206-555c-8934-c6d19566d547" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Area Between Two Curves as an Integral by First Solving For the Limits of Integration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "968c81d4-3952-5546-bf6e-2fa86c99edd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{-1}^{2/5}(-5x^{2} - 3x + 2)dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067410} {:answer/id #uuid "ffcf879e-89d4-552d-841b-265a1abe4bdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{-5}^{2}(2 - x^{2} - 4x)dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067411} {:answer/id #uuid "1d25566a-112d-528b-8bb3-9ee467995089" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{-5}^{2}(4x^{2} - x)dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067412} {:answer/id #uuid "55169664-5c10-50fa-bb89-ca3fd3db0275" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{-1}^{2/5}(2 - x^{2} - 4x)dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067413} {:answer/id #uuid "77e1737d-81b3-5bac-be4d-95f8051dd21e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{-1}^{2/5}(4x^{2} - x)dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067414}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "968c81d4-3952-5546-bf6e-2fa86c99edd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{-1}^{2/5}(-5x^{2} - 3x + 2)dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067410} :answer-field/id #uuid "1961923d-7ea2-5d20-b11d-3cfa8db69ef6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067409}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d663ded3-4e3e-5b80-80de-bcbc2d02a27f" :question/math-academy-id "q-49885" :question/problem "Which integral gives the area of the finite region bounded by $y=4{x}^{2}-x$ and $y=2-{x}^{2}-4x?$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw a sketch of the two functions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/913ec93445f3ed571fcf98ad762eba2a1bc657dce7442c8e48fc4d5f36817def.png)\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-x & =2-{x}^{2}-4x \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+3x-2 & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{-3\\pm \\sqrt{{3}^{2}-4\\cdot 5\\cdot (-2)}}{2\\cdot 5} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3\\pm 7}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-1$ to $x=\\frac{2}{5}.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/77493917a13269c78acef8ff6fe57b5f350020542805e316f82f7a17dabedd24.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=4{x}^{2}-x,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=2-{x}^{2}-4x.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2/5}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2/5}[\\,(2-{x}^{2}-4x)-(4{x}^{2}-x)\\,]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2/5}(-5{x}^{2}-3x+2)\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291565 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee633017-5c17-575f-bdab-5f12b7319355" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Area Between Two Curves as an Integral Given the Limits of Integration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63538978-4f38-5531-9422-4a476769e4fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{2}+x+2)\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291567} {:answer/id #uuid "4486de62-2bc6-5471-a3f1-644e486e08b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}(-{x}^{2}-x+2)\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291568} {:answer/id #uuid "d385054a-cc01-50f3-af7b-5b1ce418da9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{2}({x}^{2}+2x+2)\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291569} {:answer/id #uuid "954c431b-f940-55d1-8afe-2e5e8066a275" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}(-{x}^{2}+x+2)\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291570} {:answer/id #uuid "614040ab-a878-52cf-a7c5-d5da620f21c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}({x}^{2}-x-2)\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291571}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "954c431b-f940-55d1-8afe-2e5e8066a275" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}(-{x}^{2}+x+2)\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291570} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c0f4119-12bf-54de-878a-5a327efe771c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291566}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bfae9b36-1a8c-554d-b305-756cecdba2e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-80186" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/c77f7a71ccb7b4ad1598b09bf4fe585c82a7f614ebb4abe3f495643a746fcb3c.png)\n\nWhich integral gives the area of the finite region bounded by $y=-x$ and $y=2-{x}^{2},$ shown above?\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the intersection points between the two graphs occur when $x=-1$ and $x=2.$ So these will be our limits of integration.\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/9e6e1147d95c65465c802e8bfad5c4ac285dc9716d098452c41bdd6caed5b9ca.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=-x,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=2-{x}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}[\\,(2-{x}^{2})-(-x)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}(-{x}^{2}+x+2)\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
